const title = "8 Best Impact Wrenches for Lug Nuts, Suspension and Seized Bolts";
const description =
  "Eight impact wrenches compared across high-torque, mid-torque and compact classes, plus why breakaway torque is a marketing number and how much you really need.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "milwaukee-2967-20",
    "name": "Milwaukee M18 FUEL 1/2\" High Torque Impact Wrench with Friction Ring (2967-20)",
    "category": "1/2\" High-Torque, 18V Brushless",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 1900,
    "pros": [
      "Rated 1,200 ft-lbs fastening and 1,600 ft-lbs nut-busting, enough for seized suspension and truck hardware",
      "About 7.6 inches long, short enough to fit places older high-torque wrenches could not",
      "4-mode Drive Control including an auto shut-off mode that stops at a light snug for reassembly",
      "Friction ring anvil allows fast one-handed socket swaps",
      "Runs on the widest pro battery platform in North America"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Around 6 lbs bare and more with a large pack, which you feel overhead",
      "Far more tool than a homeowner rotating tires on a sedan needs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The high-torque wrench to buy if you regularly fight rust, trucks or equipment, and the one that proves high torque no longer has to mean a long, clumsy tool.",
    "description": "The 2967 is the tool that closed most of the gap between cordless and air. Milwaukee rates it at 1,200 ft-lbs of fastening torque and 1,600 ft-lbs of nut-busting torque, and while those figures are best-case numbers measured with a big battery, the practical result is that this wrench removes rusted axle nuts, control-arm bolts and trailer hardware that stall mid-torque tools. What makes it our top pick rather than the most powerful tool on the list is the package: at roughly 7.6 inches long it is short enough to get behind a wheel hub or into a wheel well at an angle, which matters more in real work than a few hundred extra rated foot-pounds. The four-mode Drive Control is genuinely useful. Mode 1 is gentle enough to run small fasteners without snapping them, and the auto shut-off mode stops impacting almost immediately after a nut seats, which keeps you from hammering lug nuts to three times their spec. Bolt removal control slows the tool once a fastener breaks free so nuts do not go flying across the shop. The limitation is the same one every high-torque wrench has: it is heavier than you need for tire rotations and brake jobs, and on overhead work the weight adds up quickly. If most of your work is passenger cars, look at the mid-torque 2962 first."
  },
  {
    "id": "milwaukee-2962-20",
    "name": "Milwaukee M18 FUEL 1/2\" Mid-Torque Impact Wrench with Friction Ring (2962-20)",
    "category": "1/2\" Mid-Torque, 18V Brushless",
    "badge": "Best for Most Car Owners",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.8,
    "reviewCount": 2600,
    "pros": [
      "Rated 650 ft-lbs, which covers lug nuts and most suspension bolts on cars and light trucks",
      "About 6 inches long and roughly 5 lbs with a battery, easy to hold one-handed",
      "Four drive modes including auto shut-off and bolt removal control",
      "Friction ring anvil for quick socket changes",
      "Pin-detent version (2962P-20) available for people who drop sockets overhead"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Will stall on large, badly rusted fasteners that the 2967 handles",
      "Premium price for a mid-torque tool compared with Ryobi or store brands"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The impact wrench most people should buy: enough torque for every lug nut on a passenger vehicle, in a tool light enough to use all afternoon.",
    "description": "If you are buying an impact wrench mainly to rotate tires, swap winter wheels and do brake and suspension jobs on cars and light SUVs, this is the one we would buy. Milwaukee rates the second-generation 2962 at 650 ft-lbs, and passenger-car lug nuts are typically specified somewhere around 80 to 140 ft-lbs, so even a stubborn, over-tightened nut from a tire shop is well within its reach. The payoff for choosing mid-torque over high-torque is size and weight. The 2962 is about 6 inches long and roughly 5 lbs with a 5.0Ah battery, which means you can hold it one-handed at arm's length while your other hand steadies the wheel. That is the difference between a tool you reach for and one that stays in the case. The four-mode Drive Control is the same system as on the high-torque model, with an auto shut-off setting for reassembly that runs nuts down and stops before it can over-tighten them. It is not a finishing tool: you still torque lug nuts with a click-type wrench. Where the 2962 runs out of road is large rusted fasteners on trucks and older equipment; if that describes a lot of your work, spend the extra on the 2967. For everyone else, the extra torque would only add weight."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dcf961b",
    "name": "DeWalt 20V MAX XR 1/2\" High Torque Impact Wrench with Hog Ring Anvil (DCF961B)",
    "category": "1/2\" High-Torque, 20V MAX Brushless",
    "badge": "Most Rated Torque",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 900,
    "pros": [
      "DeWalt rates it at up to 1,200 ft-lbs fastening and 1,750 ft-lbs breakaway",
      "Automatic power boost after several seconds of impacting helps free stubborn fasteners",
      "Precision Wrench mode limits over-tightening in forward and run-off in reverse",
      "Hog ring anvil holds impact sockets securely",
      "Natural choice for anyone already on DeWalt 20V batteries"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Noticeably longer and heavier than the Milwaukee 2967",
      "Peak ratings assume DeWalt's largest high-output packs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The DeWalt owner's high-torque wrench, with the biggest rated numbers on this list and the size and weight that come with them.",
    "description": "DeWalt pitched the DCF961 as the highest-rated cordless 1/2-inch impact wrench, and on paper it earns that: the company rates it at up to 1,200 ft-lbs fastening and 1,750 ft-lbs breakaway, with even higher figures quoted when paired with its newest PowerStack battery. That detail is worth dwelling on, because it shows exactly how these numbers work. Peak torque depends heavily on the pack attached, so the rating printed on the box is only reachable with the largest, most expensive battery in the range. With an ordinary 5.0Ah pack the tool is still very strong, but you should not expect to see the headline figure. In use, the feature that helps most is the automatic power boost: after a few seconds of impacting on a fastener that will not move, the tool raises its output, which is exactly when you want it. Precision Wrench mode is DeWalt's answer to Milwaukee's auto shut-off and works well for running nuts on without over-tightening them. The trade-off is bulk. The DCF961 is longer and heavier than the Milwaukee 2967, which matters inside a wheel well or under a truck. If you already own DeWalt 20V batteries and need high torque, this is the tool to buy. If you do not own either platform, the more compact Milwaukee is the better tool."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dcf891b",
    "name": "DeWalt 20V MAX XR 1/2\" Mid-Range Impact Wrench with Hog Ring Anvil (DCF891B)",
    "category": "1/2\" Mid-Torque, 20V MAX Brushless",
    "badge": "Best Mid-Torque for DeWalt Users",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 3800,
    "pros": [
      "Rated 600 ft-lbs fastening and 800 ft-lbs breakaway, plenty for passenger-car lug nuts",
      "Around 4 lbs bare, one of the lightest 1/2-inch wrenches here",
      "Precision Wrench mode for controlled reassembly",
      "4-mode push-button speed selector",
      "Widely sold bare and in kits at every big-box store"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not the answer for heavily rusted truck or equipment hardware",
      "Push-button mode selector is easy to bump by accident"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The DeWalt equivalent of Milwaukee's mid-torque wrench, and the right buy for DeWalt owners who work mostly on cars.",
    "description": "The DCF891 is DeWalt's mid-range 1/2-inch wrench, rated at 600 ft-lbs of fastening torque and 800 ft-lbs of breakaway, and it plays the same role on the yellow platform that the 2962 plays on the red one. That breakaway figure is several times what a passenger-car lug nut should be tightened to, so as long as your wheels were not installed by someone leaning on a 4-foot breaker bar, this wrench removes them without drama. At around 4 lbs bare it is light, and with a 5.0Ah battery it stays comfortable for a full set of four wheels plus brakes. The four-mode selector includes Precision Wrench, which slows the tool as a fastener seats in forward and slows it again as it breaks free in reverse, so nuts do not spin off and disappear under the car. The hog ring anvil holds impact sockets firmly; you will need a pick or a firm pull to remove them, which some people prefer to a friction ring. The only real complaint in daily use is that the push-button mode selector can be bumped without noticing, so check the setting before you run lug nuts on. If you are on DeWalt batteries already, buy this before you consider the much heavier DCF961. Most people will never miss the difference."
  },
  {
    "id": "milwaukee-2562-20",
    "name": "Milwaukee M12 FUEL Stubby 3/8\" Impact Wrench (2562-20)",
    "category": "3/8\" Compact Stubby, 12V Brushless",
    "badge": "Best Compact",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 600,
    "pros": [
      "Rated 400 ft-lbs fastening and 550 ft-lbs nut-busting from a 12V tool",
      "About 4.8 inches long and roughly 2.2 lbs bare, fits where 18V wrenches cannot",
      "3/8-inch drive suits the socket sizes most engine-bay work uses",
      "Enough output for many lug nuts on small cars in a pinch",
      "1/2-inch version (2563-20) exists if you want one socket set for everything"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Small 12V packs drain quickly on heavy, repeated breakaway work",
      "Not a substitute for a 1/2-inch wrench on large rusted fasteners"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The wrench that actually lives in a mechanic's hand all day, and the best second impact wrench you can buy.",
    "description": "Milwaukee's M12 FUEL stubby has been the reference compact impact wrench for years, and the current 2562-20 replaces the older 2554 with a large jump in output: Milwaukee rates it at 400 ft-lbs of fastening torque and 550 ft-lbs of nut-busting, from a tool that is only about 4.8 inches long and roughly 2.2 lbs bare. Those numbers put it in the territory that full-size 18V mid-torque wrenches occupied not long ago. The point of the stubby is not raw power, though. It is access. Engine-bay bolts, brake caliper brackets, exhaust hangers, seat bolts and anything behind a fender liner are all easier with a tool this short, and 3/8-inch drive is the right size for the 10mm to 19mm fasteners that dominate modern cars. It has the familiar four-mode Drive Control with an auto shut-off setting, so you can run bolts down quickly and finish them by hand with a torque wrench. We would not make this anyone's only impact wrench if they deal with trucks or rusted suspension, because 12V packs run down fast on repeated heavy breakaways. As a companion to a 1/2-inch tool, or as the only wrench for someone doing light maintenance on small cars, it is excellent. If you would rather keep one socket set, the 2563-20 is the same tool with a 1/2-inch anvil."
  },
  {
    "id": "ryobi-pbliw01",
    "name": "Ryobi ONE+ HP 18V Brushless 4-Mode 1/2\" High Torque Impact Wrench (PBLIW01)",
    "category": "1/2\" High-Torque, 18V Brushless",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 2500,
    "pros": [
      "Ryobi rates it at 1,170 ft-lbs breakaway and 700 ft-lbs fastening",
      "Costs much less than pro-brand high-torque wrenches",
      "Assist mode stops rotation after a few impacts to help avoid over-tightening",
      "Friction ring anvil for quick socket changes",
      "Runs on the same ONE+ batteries as hundreds of homeowner tools"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Larger and heavier than Milwaukee's high-torque wrench for similar rated output",
      "Needs Ryobi's larger HP batteries to perform like its rating suggests"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best value if you already own Ryobi batteries and want a wrench that will never struggle with lug nuts.",
    "description": "For a homeowner who already owns Ryobi ONE+ batteries, this is the easiest recommendation on the page. Ryobi rates the PBLIW01 at 1,170 ft-lbs of breakaway torque and 700 ft-lbs of fastening torque, which is well into high-torque territory, for considerably less money than the pro brands. In practice, that means every lug nut on a car, SUV or half-ton pickup comes off without hesitation, and most suspension and trailer hardware does too. There are four modes, including an Assist mode that stops the anvil after a handful of impacts once a nut seats, which is helpful for running lug nuts down without hammering them past spec. The friction ring anvil makes socket swaps quick. The compromises are real but acceptable at this price. The tool is bigger and heavier than a Milwaukee 2967 with comparable ratings, which makes a difference in tight wheel wells, and it needs one of Ryobi's larger HP packs to deliver its full output; with a small 2.0Ah battery it feels noticeably weaker. Ryobi also does not have the pro-grade accessory ecosystem, but for a garage mechanic that rarely matters. If you are choosing a platform from scratch for automotive work only, a mid-torque Milwaukee or DeWalt is the better long-term tool. If you are already on Ryobi, buy this and a good 5-piece impact socket set."
  },
  {
    "id": "makita-xwt08z",
    "name": "Makita 18V LXT Brushless High Torque 1/2\" Square Drive Impact Wrench (XWT08Z)",
    "category": "1/2\" High-Torque, 18V LXT Brushless",
    "badge": "Best for Makita Users",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 1400,
    "pros": [
      "Rated 740 ft-lbs fastening and 1,180 ft-lbs nut-busting",
      "Three-speed power selection for matching output to the fastener",
      "Extreme Protection Technology for better dust and water resistance",
      "Built-in LED lighting for dark wheel wells and undercarriages",
      "Runs on Makita's widely owned 18V LXT batteries"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Heaviest cordless wrench on this list",
      "Older design without the auto shut-off modes Milwaukee and DeWalt offer"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A durable, strong high-torque wrench for people already invested in Makita LXT, but not one to switch platforms for.",
    "description": "The XWT08Z has been Makita's 18V high-torque 1/2-inch wrench for several years, and it is still a strong tool: Makita rates it at 740 ft-lbs of maximum fastening torque and 1,180 ft-lbs of nut-busting torque, which is more than enough for automotive work and most light-truck hardware. The three-speed power selector is simple and effective, and Makita's Extreme Protection Technology is a genuine advantage if the tool will live in a farm truck, on a trailer or anywhere dust and rain are part of the job. The LED lights are placed well for undercarriage work. Where it shows its age is in features and size. It lacks the auto shut-off or precision modes that Milwaukee and DeWalt now use to stop a wrench from over-tightening fasteners, so you have to control the trigger yourself when running nuts on, and it is the heaviest cordless wrench on this list. For overhead work or long days, that weight adds up. The honest recommendation is simple: if you own Makita LXT batteries, buy this before you buy into a second platform, because it will handle anything a homeowner or small shop throws at it. If you do not, the Milwaukee 2967 is more compact, more modern and has better control features for similar money."
  },
  {
    "id": "ingersoll-rand-2235timax",
    "name": "Ingersoll Rand 2235TiMAX 1/2\" Drive Air Impact Wrench",
    "category": "1/2\" Pneumatic, Titanium Hammer Case",
    "badge": "Best Air Impact Wrench",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 3200,
    "pros": [
      "Rated 1,350 ft-lbs nut-busting and up to 930 ft-lbs in reverse",
      "About 4.6 lbs, lighter than any cordless high-torque wrench here",
      "Titanium hammer case keeps weight down without giving up durability",
      "No batteries to charge or replace, runs as long as the compressor keeps up",
      "Decades-old design with parts and rebuild kits widely available"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs a compressor that can keep up, plus 3/8-inch hose and fittings",
      "Loud, and the hose limits where you can take it"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Still the benchmark air wrench, and the right pick if you already have a capable compressor in the garage.",
    "description": "The 2235TiMAX is the air wrench most professional shops standardized on, and it remains the benchmark for a reason. Ingersoll Rand rates it at 1,350 ft-lbs of nut-busting torque and up to 930 ft-lbs of working torque in reverse, from a tool that weighs around 4.6 lbs thanks to its titanium hammer case. That power-to-weight ratio is still something cordless tools only match with larger, heavier bodies and big batteries. It also runs indefinitely: there is no battery to go flat, and the twin-hammer mechanism has been refined over decades, with parts and rebuild kits easy to find. The catch is everything outside the tool. To perform like its rating, it needs 90 psi at the tool with enough volume behind it, which means a real garage compressor rather than a small pancake unit, plus a 3/8-inch hose and quality fittings, since restrictive quick-connects sap much of its output. It is loud, noticeably louder than cordless wrenches, so hearing protection is not optional. And the hose keeps you tethered to the garage. If you already own a capable compressor and do most of your work in one bay, this is the most power per dollar and per pound you can buy. If you do not, the cost of the compressor usually makes a cordless wrench the smarter choice."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Impact Wrenches",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-impact-wrench/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size impact wrench do I need for lug nuts?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 1/2-inch drive mid-torque wrench rated around 500 to 800 ft-lbs of breakaway torque handles lug nuts on virtually every car, crossover and half-ton pickup. Passenger-car lug nuts are typically specified between about 80 and 140 ft-lbs. Step up to high-torque only if you work on heavy trucks or deal with heavily rusted wheels regularly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is breakaway torque the same as fastening torque?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Fastening torque is the maximum a wrench can tighten in forward. Breakaway, or nut-busting, torque is the maximum it can apply to loosen a fastener in reverse and is always the larger number. Both are best-case figures measured by the manufacturer, usually with the largest battery, so real-world output will be lower."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use an impact wrench to tighten lug nuts?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can use it to run lug nuts down, ideally on the lowest setting or an auto shut-off mode, but never to do the final tightening. An impact wrench can overshoot the specified torque several times over. Finish every nut with a click-type torque wrench set to your vehicle's specification, working in a star pattern."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a cordless impact wrench as good as an air impact wrench?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most home users, yes. Modern brushless 18V and 20V wrenches match or beat typical air wrenches on real fasteners and need no compressor. Air still wins on power-to-weight and unlimited runtime, and it is cheaper if you already own a large compressor. Starting from nothing, cordless is usually the better investment."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need impact sockets?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Standard chrome sockets are brittle and can crack or shatter under an impact wrench's hammer blows. Impact sockets are made from tougher chrome-molybdenum steel with thicker walls and are designed to absorb impacts safely. Choose six-point impact sockets in the drive size that matches your wrench, and avoid stacking adapters."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between a friction ring and a pin detent anvil?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A friction ring holds sockets with a spring ring and allows fast one-handed socket changes, which suits most automotive work. A pin detent locks the socket in place with a spring-loaded pin that engages a hole in the socket. It is slower to change but will not drop a socket when working overhead or at awkward angles."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can an impact driver remove lug nuts?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not well. Impact drivers are designed for screws and small fasteners and typically produce under 200 ft-lbs of torque. With a hex-to-square adapter, a strong driver might loosen a lightly tightened lug nut, but the adapter can snap and the tool will struggle. If you work on vehicles regularly, buy a proper impact wrench."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best impact wrench",
      "cordless impact wrench",
      "1/2 inch impact wrench",
      "impact wrench for lug nuts",
      "mid-torque impact wrench",
      "air impact wrench"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            8 Wrenches Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Impact Wrenches</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Most people buy twice the torque they need and carry the extra weight on every job. Here is how to pick the right class first, then the right tool.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#milwaukee-2967-20"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            How to Choose
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
