        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started from the jobs people actually buy an impact wrench for, not from the spec sheet. For most readers that means removing and installing lug nuts, followed by brake work, suspension bolts, and the occasional seized fastener on a trailer, mower deck or old truck. A smaller group works on farm equipment, heavy trucks or rusted-out project cars, and they need a very different tool. Every wrench on this list earns its place by being the best fit for one of those groups.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted size and weight far more heavily than peak torque. In our hands-on use, the tool that comes off the shelf most often is the one that fits behind a strut and can be held one-handed, not the one with the biggest number. A wrench that stays in its case because it is too heavy to bother with is worth less than a lighter one that gets used every weekend.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also looked hard at control features. Modern brushless wrenches offer auto shut-off or precision modes that stop impacting as soon as a nut seats. These do not replace a torque wrench, but they stop you from hammering a lug nut to three times its spec, which is how wheel studs get stretched and brake rotors get warped. Tools with good control modes scored higher than tools that rely entirely on your trigger finger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we considered the battery platform. An impact wrench is rarely a standalone purchase. If you already own Milwaukee, DeWalt, Makita or Ryobi batteries, the best wrench for you is usually the best one on that platform, which is why this list includes a strong option for each. We excluded no-name wrenches with inflated torque claims that we could not trust, and we only list tools that are currently sold and easy to find.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="torque-classes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. High-Torque vs Mid-Torque vs Compact
          </h2>

          <p>
            Impact wrenches fall into three practical classes, and choosing the class matters more than choosing the brand. High-torque 1/2-inch wrenches are rated from roughly 1,000 to well over 1,500 ft-lbs of breakaway torque. They exist for large, rusted or heavily torqued fasteners: truck axle nuts, heavy suspension bolts, farm implements and anything that has been sitting in salt for a decade. They are the heaviest and most expensive class.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mid-torque 1/2-inch wrenches are typically rated from about 500 to 800 ft-lbs. That sounds modest next to the high-torque figures, but it is several times what any passenger-car lug nut should be tightened to. Mid-torque tools are shorter, lighter and easier to control, and for rotating tires, swapping wheels and doing brakes and suspension on cars and crossovers they are the right answer. This is the class most homeowners should buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compact wrenches, usually 3/8-inch drive and often 12V, are built for access rather than raw power. They slip into engine bays, behind fender liners and under dashboards where no 18V tool fits. The newest ones are surprisingly strong, and Milwaukee's current M12 stubby is rated at 550 ft-lbs of nut-busting torque, but their small batteries drain quickly under heavy use. They make an excellent second wrench and a reasonable first wrench for light work on small cars.
          </p>

