        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most people should buy a mid-torque 1/2-inch wrench on the battery platform they already own. The Milwaukee 2962 and DeWalt DCF891 handle every lug nut on a passenger vehicle and are light enough that you will actually reach for them. Step up to the Milwaukee 2967 or DeWalt DCF961 only if rusted truck and equipment hardware is a regular part of your life.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, treat the torque rating as a class label rather than a promise, pair the wrench with a large battery and impact-rated six-point sockets, and finish every wheel with a torque wrench. That combination gets you the speed of an impact wrench without the stretched studs and warped rotors that come from letting it do the final tightening.
          </p>
